  I found no High or Medium issues. There are three Low findings, two of them about the upcoming nginx config. I did not read `.env`.
  
  | Severity | File:line | Issue | Fix |
  |---|---|---|---|
  | Low | /Users/vbb/IdeaProjects/creditsAI/docker-compose.yml:84 | `KC_PROXY_HEADERS: xforwarded` makes Keycloak trust `X-Forwarded-*` from any caller. Today only containers on the compose network can reach it, because there is no published port, so the exposure is small. Two gaps remain. First, if nginx sends `X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for`, it appends to whatever the client sent, so a client can fake the IP Keycloak records in events and uses for brute-force detection. Second, no trusted-proxy restriction is set. Spoofed Host/Proto has limited impact because `KC_HOSTNAME` is fixed. | In the coming `frontend/nginx.conf`, set `proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $remote_addr;` and also set Host, X-Forwarded-Proto and X-Forwarded-Host explicitly. Optionally add `KC_PROXY_TRUSTED_ADDRESSES` set to the compose subnet. |
  | Low | /Users/vbb/IdeaProjects/creditsAI/backend/src/main/java/com/creditsai/security/SecurityConfig.java:110-114 | This fails closed, but it can break the app depending on nginx. Browsers send `Origin` even on same-origin POST/PUT/PATCH/DELETE. If nginx uses plain `proxy_pass http://backend:8080` without forwarding Host, Tomcat sees host `backend:8080`. `http://localhost` then counts as cross-origin and every write gets 403 from the empty origin list. This is not a vulnerability. | In the `/api/` location, add `proxy_set_header Host $host;`, or enable `server.forward-headers-strategy` in prod. Add an end-to-end check that a POST with `Origin: http://localhost` succeeds through nginx. |
  | Low (existed before this change) | /Users/vbb/IdeaProjects/creditsAI/docker-compose.yml:12 and keycloak/import/credits-realm.json:41,51 | The prod Keycloak shares the `x-keycloak` anchor, so it also runs `start-dev`. The realm import also gives prod the test users `user1`/`user1` and `admin1`/`admin1`. The rules call these users "dev only". This is fine for a localhost course stack but should not be called production-ready. | Accept and document this, or run `start` with a realm import that has no test users for prod. |
  
  ## Your focus questions
  - **Empty CORS list:** safe. With an empty `allowedOrigins`, Spring's `DefaultCorsProcessor` matches no origin. Any request with a different `Origin` gets 403 and no `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header. `*` is still rejected at startup, and `ProdProfileTest` checks the 403 on a preflight. Requests without an `Origin` header, such as same-origin GETs or curl, still have to pass JWT authentication, so the empty list opens nothing.
  - **Prod profile importing `.env` or keeping dev settings:** no. `spring.config.import` exists only in `application-dev.yml`. `application.yml` holds only shared, mode-neutral settings, and the datasource password is `${CREDITS_DB_PASSWORD}`. Compose sets `SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE=prod`, which overrides `spring.profiles.default=dev`. The image build context is `./backend`, so the root `.env` can't be copied into the image. Inside the container, `../.env` from `/app` points to `/.env`, which doesn't exist. If the profile variable were ever dropped, the backend would start as dev with localhost URLs and fail to connect: it fails closed and exposes nothing. Optionally add `.env*` to `backend/.dockerignore` as defense in depth.
  - **Secrets:** none found in compose or the YAML files. All passwords use `${VAR:?...}` or `${VAR}`. The root `.gitignore` excludes `.env` and `.env.*` but keeps `.env.example`. The only passwords in source are the documented test-user credentials in the realm JSON.
  
